---
import Doc from '../_Doc.astro'
import { categories, statusValues, catalogSchema } from '../../../lib/extensions-docs.js'

const cats = categories()
const statuses = statusValues()
const entry = catalogSchema().$defs.entry

const LAYOUT = `extensions/
  clackpad/
    catalog.json          one entry per extension: dir, status, noncommercial, category, tags, screenshots
    index.json            generated: what the app reads
    store.json            generated: what asystemofcells.com renders
    dist/                 generated: <id>-<version>.cpx, committed; keep older versions
    <your-extension>/     extension.json, icon.png, README.md, LICENSE, NOTICE.md, CHANGELOG.md`
const ENTRY = `{
  "dir": "my-extension",
  "status": "community",
  "noncommercial": true,
  "category": "productivity",
  "tags": ["notes", "obsidian"],
  "screenshots": ["my-extension/shots/tab.png"]
}`
const CHANGELOG = `## 1.1.0
- Added a daily-note mode.

## 1.0.0
- First release.`
const CMDS = `node extensions/sdk/validate.mjs extensions/clackpad/my-extension
node extensions/tools/build.mjs clackpad            # validate, pack, rewrite dist/, index.json, store.json
node extensions/tools/build.mjs clackpad --check    # change nothing; exit 1 if anything is stale
pnpm test`
const STATUS = {
  official: 'Made by the Clackpad maintainer. The app believes it only when the catalog came from the default source; from any other source it is shown as community.',
  community: 'Independent and reviewed.',
  'fan-made': 'A tribute or imitation of a third-party product; not affiliated with it. Must be marked noncommercial, free, and described as unaffiliated.',
}
---
<Doc
  title="Publish an extension"
  description="How to submit a Clackpad extension to the asystemofcells catalog: pull request layout, the build check, version policy, status values, catalog metadata, curation policy, reviewer checklist and removal policy."
  path="/extensions/publish/"
  eyebrow="Extensions / Process"
  heading="Publish an extension"
  lead="Submission is a pull request. The catalog is static files in a repository; there is no account, no upload form and no telemetry."
  sources={['extensions/tools/build.mjs', 'extensions/sdk/schemas/catalog.schema.json', 'extensions/README.md']}
>
  <h2 id="submit">How to submit</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Fork <a href="https://github.com/mbaliga/asystemofcells">mbaliga/asystemofcells</a>. <b>One extension per pull request.</b></li>
    <li>Put your extension in <code>extensions/&lt;app&gt;/&lt;your-extension&gt;/</code> (for Clackpad: <code>extensions/clackpad/</code>).</li>
    <li>Add one entry for it to that app&rsquo;s <code>catalog.json</code> (fields below).</li>
    <li>Run the build. It validates every extension, packs each one deterministically, and rewrites <code>dist/</code>, <code>index.json</code> and <code>store.json</code>. Commit them.</li>
    <li>Run the check. CI runs the same <code>--check</code> on every pull request that touches <code>extensions/</code>, and a failing check is not reviewed further.</li>
    <li>Describe the change in the pull request. For <code>typed:read</code> or any <code>net:</code> permission, add a one-line justification.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>{CMDS}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{LAYOUT}</code></pre>
  <p class="note">
    Older Clackpad documentation describes adding a hand-written entry (with a package URL and sha256) to <code>index.json</code>. In this repository
    <code>index.json</code> and <code>store.json</code> are generated by <code>build.mjs</code> from <code>catalog.json</code> and the extension folders, and the package URL points at
    <code>dist/</code> in this repository. Follow the process on this page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metadata">Catalog metadata the build requires</h2>
  <p>Each entry in <code>catalog.json</code> is read by <code>build.mjs</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{ENTRY}</code></pre>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="catalog.json entry fields">
    <table class="dense-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Rule</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>dir</code></td><td>The extension folder, relative to the app folder. It must pass the validator or the build fails.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>category</code></td><td><b>Required.</b> One of: {cats.map((c, i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ', ' : ''}<code>{c}</code></>))}. The build fails otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>One of: {statuses.map((c, i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ', ' : ''}<code>{c}</code></>))} (the catalog schema&rsquo;s enum). Maintainers set it, not authors; see below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>noncommercial</code></td><td>Boolean; <code>true</code> for free fan work. Shown as a label.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td>Optional array of short strings; defaults to none.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>screenshots</code></td><td>Optional array of paths relative to the app folder (for example <code>my-extension/shots/tab.png</code>). Each file must exist or the build fails. They are published as URLs under the catalog base. Use your own screenshots; no third-party logos or trademarks.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the extension folder the build also reads <code>README.md</code> (the first 8000 characters are shown in the store) and <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>. The changelog is parsed
    by its <code>## &lt;version&gt;</code> headings, with each bullet becoming a note:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{CHANGELOG}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Everything else in the entry (<code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>permissions</code>, <code>minClackpad</code>, licence, homepage) is taken from your
    <code>extension.json</code>, so the catalog and the package cannot disagree. Entry limits from the catalog schema: <code>name</code> {entry.properties.name.maxLength}, <code>author</code> {entry.properties.author.maxLength}, <code>description</code> {entry.properties.description.maxLength} characters.
  </p>
  <p>
    The build does not itself check that <code>status</code> is one of the three values, that <code>fan-made</code> entries are <code>noncommercial</code>, that a <code>LICENSE</code> file exists, or that a package is under 5 MB (the validator enforces the package size). Reviewers check those by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="versions">Version policy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bump <code>version</code> in <code>extension.json</code> for every change, add a line to <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>, run the build, commit <code>dist/</code>, <code>index.json</code> and <code>store.json</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Never reuse a version number with different bytes.</b> A version must be higher than the previous entry&rsquo;s. Keep older <code>.cpx</code> files in <code>dist/</code>; users on older builds keep the older version.</li>
    <li>Patch / minor / major meanings, <code>minClackpad</code> policy, and the rule that a new permission is at least a minor bump are on <a href="/extensions/format/#versioning">Manifest format</a>.</li>
    <li>An update that adds a permission is reviewed again from scratch, not as a diff.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="status">Status values</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Status values">
    <table class="dense-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{statuses.map((s) => (<tr><td><code>{s}</code></td><td>{STATUS[s]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><code>status</code> is the maintainers&rsquo; claim, not the author&rsquo;s: <code>official</code> only for the Clackpad maintainer&rsquo;s own, <code>community</code> once reviewed, <code>fan-made</code> per the rule above. Authors do not choose it.</p>

  <h2 id="curation">Curation policy</h2>
  <p>An entry is accepted when all of these hold:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>It validates.</b> The validator passes and the entry&rsquo;s sha256, id, version and permissions match the package (the build guarantees the match).</li>
    <li><b>Least permission.</b> Each permission is used by something visible in the manifest. <code>typed:read</code> and <code>net:*</code> need a one-line justification in the PR. See <a href="/extensions/permissions/">Permissions</a>.</li>
    <li><b>No bundled third-party IP.</b> No trademarks, logos, fonts, sounds, dictionaries or code the author cannot redistribute; the licence is stated and the licence text ships in the package.</li>
    <li><b>Honest naming.</b> No use of another company&rsquo;s name or logo as the extension&rsquo;s identity. Imitation of a product&rsquo;s behaviour is <code>fan-made</code> and <code>noncommercial</code>, free, and described as unaffiliated.</li>
    <li><b>No surprises.</b> The description says what it does; sandboxed pages do what the description says; any <code>net:</code> host is the service the description names.</li>
    <li><b>Maintained.</b> A working homepage or repository and an author who can be contacted.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="checklist">Reviewer checklist</h2>
  <h3>1. Mechanical (the machine does it)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The validator passes, and <code>node extensions/tools/build.mjs clackpad --check</code> passes.</li>
    <li><code>version</code> is higher than the previous entry&rsquo;s, and no version number is reused with different bytes. Older <code>.cpx</code> files stay published.</li>
    <li>The package is at most 5 MB. <code>minClackpad</code> is the oldest build that has what is used.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Permissions (the human does this)</h3>
  <p>Read the manifest next to the description; for each permission ask whether something visible needs it. The per-permission table is on <a href="/extensions/permissions/#least-permission-what-reviewers-expect">Permissions</a>. An update that adds a permission is reviewed from scratch.</p>
  <h3>3. Pages (anything in <code>pages/</code>)</h3>
  <p>Sandboxed pages are the only third-party code. Read them. Refuse or ask for changes when a page:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>fetches from a host it does not need, sends clipboard or typed text anywhere other than the declared service, or builds URLs from data it was not given;</li>
    <li>loads remote scripts (the sandbox blocks most of this, but it still shows intent), uses <code>eval</code> or <code>new Function</code>, or hides behaviour in minified or obfuscated code (ask for source);</li>
    <li>does something the description does not say. Pages do what the description says, nothing more.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>4. Content and naming</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No bundled third-party trademarks, logos, fonts, sounds, dictionaries or code the author cannot redistribute; a <code>license</code> is stated and the licence text ships in the package.</li>
    <li>The name and icon do not suggest the extension is made or endorsed by a company the author is not. Fan work says it is unaffiliated.</li>
    <li><code>status</code> is set by the maintainers, per above.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>5. Decision</h3>
  <p>Accept, ask for changes (say which numbered point), or decline with the reason.</p>

  <h2 id="removal">Takedown and removal</h2>
  <p>
    An entry may be removed without notice for malware, misleading naming, bundled third-party assets, or a takedown request from a rights holder. A removed entry stops appearing in
    Browse; already-installed copies keep working until the user removes them (the app never auto-updates or auto-removes).
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Legal and IP note.</b> Extensions must not ship third-party keyboard layouts, logos, fonts or sounds. Describe compatibility in words (&ldquo;works with Obsidian vaults&rdquo;), not with someone
    else&rsquo;s logo or name in your own name or icon. The app also lets users install from anywhere, at their own risk, with a stronger warning.
  </p>
</Doc>
